.TH DENET 1 "November 2026" "denet" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
denet \- a streaming process monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B denet
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.B run
\fICOMMAND\fR...
.br
.B denet
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.B attach
\fIPID\fR
.br
.B denet stats
\fIFILE\fR
.br
.B denet stacks
\fIFILE\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B denet
tracks the CPU, memory, disk I/O, threads and network bytes of a process and
its children while it runs, and reports them as a live line, as JSON records,
or as full-screen graphs. Sampling is adaptive: it starts at
.B \-\-interval
and backs off to
.BR \-\-max\-interval .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI run " COMMAND" ...
Run
.I COMMAND
and monitor it until it exits. Put
.B \-\-
before a command that has options of its own.
.TP
.BI attach " PID"
Monitor a process that is already running.
.TP
.BI stats " FILE"
Print summary statistics from a metrics file written with
.BR \-\-out .
.TP
.BI stacks " FILE"
Print the off-CPU waits of a metrics file recorded with
.B \-\-enable\-ebpf
as folded stacks, one line per command and kernel stack with the milliseconds
waited there: the input of flamegraph tools such as
.BR inferno\-flamegraph .
.SH OPTIONS
Options go before the command.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-json
Output JSON records, one per line.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-out " \fIFILE\fR"
Write the records to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interval " \fIMS\fR"
Base sampling interval in milliseconds (default 100).
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-max\-interval " \fIMS\fR"
Maximum sampling interval in milliseconds (default 1000).
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-duration " \fISECONDS\fR"
Stop monitoring after this many seconds (default 0, unlimited).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-update
Print a new line per sample instead of updating in place.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print nothing to standard output, except with
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.B \-\-tui
Live full-screen graphs of the process tree: CPU, memory, disk, TCP, and GPU
with
.BR \-\-gpu .
Press
.B q
to stop and
.B m
to change the graph style. The command's own output goes to a log file, named
on exit.
.TP
.B \-\-gpu
Monitor the GPU: NVIDIA through the driver's NVML library, or Apple Silicon
(experimental). Off by default.
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-ebpf
Enable eBPF profiling: syscall counts, off-CPU waits, per-process network
bytes. Needs root or capabilities; see
.BR CAPABILITIES .
.TP
.B \-\-exclude\-children
Monitor only the main process, not its children.
.TP
.B \-\-since\-process\-start
Report I/O since the process started instead of since monitoring started.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-polling
Do not sample; collect only at process completion.
.TP
.B \-\-write\-env
Write a host, NUMA and CPU-affinity record before the metadata.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Verbose diagnostics, mostly for eBPF.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and the optional features this binary was built with
.RB ( gpu ", " ebpf ).
.SH CAPABILITIES
Everything except
.B \-\-enable\-ebpf
works as an ordinary user.
.B \-\-enable\-ebpf
needs either root
.RB ( "sudo denet \-\-enable\-ebpf" " ...)"
or these capabilities on the binary:
.TP
.B cap_bpf
load the eBPF programs and create their maps
.TP
.B cap_perfmon
attach them to tracepoints
.TP
.B cap_dac_read_search
read the root-only tracepoint ids under /sys/kernel/tracing, and the RAPL energy counters
.PP
Packages do not set them. To set them once, as root:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo setcap cap_bpf,cap_perfmon,cap_dac_read_search=ep "$(command \-v denet)"
.fi
.RE
.PP
To check, and to remove them again:
.PP
.RS
.nf
getcap "$(command \-v denet)"
sudo setcap \-r "$(command \-v denet)"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Capabilities belong to the file: they are lost when the binary is replaced,
for example by a package upgrade, and must be set again. A binary with these
capabilities gives them to every user who can run it.
.B cap_bpf
and
.B cap_perfmon
exist since Linux 5.8; on older kernels use root.
.SH EXAMPLES
Monitor a command and save the records:
.PP
.RS
.nf
denet \-o run.jsonl run \-\- python train.py
denet stats run.jsonl
.fi
.RE
.PP
Watch a running process with the GPU, in full-screen graphs:
.PP
.RS
.nf
denet \-\-gpu \-\-tui attach 12345
.fi
.RE
.PP
JSON records on standard output, with eBPF:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo denet \-\-enable\-ebpf \-\-json run \-\- ./job.sh
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
Documentation and source: https://github.com/btraven00/denet
